Translation patch: `AuthPlugin::{from_bytes, into_owned}`, `AuthSwitchRequest::{auth_plugin, plugin_data}`

Change:
  - `from_bytes`: `if let [name @ .., 0] = name { name } else { name }` becomes `if len > 0 && name[len - 1] == 0 { &name[..len - 1] } else { name }`; the `match name { CONST => .., .. }` over the seven byte-string constants becomes an `if name == CONST { .. } else if ..` chain in the same order, ending in `AuthPlugin::Other(Cow::Borrowed(name))`.
  - `into_owned`: `Cow::Owned(name.into_owned())` becomes `Cow::Owned(cow_into_vec(name))` (the helper of patch 14).
  - `AuthSwitchRequest::auth_plugin`: `ParseBuf(bytes).parse(()).expect("infallible")` becomes `AuthPlugin::from_bytes(bytes)`.
  - `AuthSwitchRequest::plugin_data`: `[MysqlNativePassword, CachingSha2Password, Sha256Password, MysqlOldPassword].contains(&self.auth_plugin()) && let [head @ .., 0] = data` becomes `plugin_uses_c_string_scramble(auth_plugin_bytes) && len > 0 && data[len - 1] == 0`, returning `&data[..len - 1]`; the new private helper `plugin_uses_c_string_scramble(name)` strips one trailing 0 from `name` (as `from_bytes` does) and compares the result with the four plugin-name constants.
Why: Aeneas does not support slice patterns (patches 06, 49) or matching on constant byte strings (both give "Inconsistent projection"), nor `Cow::into_owned` (patch 14). `plugin_data` fails ("Unreachable"/"Internal error") when it matches on, or compares, the temporary `AuthPlugin` holding a borrow of `self`; `expect` needs `Debug for io::Error`.
Behaviour: Identical. A slice pattern `[head @ .., 0]` matches exactly the non-empty slices ending in 0, binding the slice without its last byte. Matching a `&[u8]` against constant patterns is slice equality, tried in order. `cow_into_vec` is `into_owned` for `Cow<[u8]>`. `AuthPlugin`'s `MyDeserialize` has `SIZE = None`, so `parse` is `deserialize`, which is `Ok(from_bytes(buf.eat_all()))`: the `expect` never panics and the result is `from_bytes(bytes)`. The derived `PartialEq` on `AuthPlugin` compares variants, so `contains` holds exactly when `from_bytes(name)` is one of the four unit variants, i.e. when the stripped name equals one of the four constants; `from_bytes` checks the constants in an order in which no two are equal, so the helper is the same test.

